China Playbook — Research round 4, costly end, fixes that worked, route test (13 Oct 2026)

The public record from 2025–26 shows that established makers no longer hold the costly end of the Indonesian mining fleet as a matter of course. Chinese makers have now sold whole 120 t truck and 125–300 t excavator fleets to top-tier contractors, using vendor finance and fuel-cost arguments to win them. Established makers do still hold the top end where a state buyer or a domestic-only tender picks the brand: Coal India, and to some degree data-centre gensets.

TL;DR
Costly end: weakened, not broken. In 2025 XCMG delivered 67 XDE130 120 t trucks plus 6 large excavators to Darma Henwa and Cokal, backed by IDR 800bn of vendor finance. In July 2026 SANY handed over two 300 t electric excavators to Jhonlin Baratama. In India, Coal India's 190 t dumper buying stays an "Open Domestic Tender", and no Chinese awards were found.
Fixes that worked: partly supported. JCB held about 78–79% of Indian backhoes (2021–22, Off-Highway Research). Komatsu/United Tractors kept mining leadership but saw overall Indonesian share fall to 20% in 2025 (from 26% in H1 2025). Komatsu's answer to Chinese wide-body trucks was to rebadge one (the Tonly-built HW70). That is a fix using assets it already owns, the UT dealer network, but it concedes the product.
Own the route: suggestive, not proven. SANY in Indonesia sells through a SANY-managed distributor (SANY Perkasa: 25,393 excavators since 2014; it runs the mining deals). The two Chinese-brand Indonesian dealers that are listed, Intraco (LiuGong) and Kobexindo, are loss-making or shrinking, with gross margins of about 15–17%. No clean same-product, different-route comparison exists in public data.
